          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Lymph from the penis and glans drains primarily to which group of lymph nodes?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Para-aortic lymph nodes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Para-aortic nodes receive drainage from deeper abdominal structures such as the testes, not from the penis or its overlying skin.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inguinal lymph nodes</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Penile lymph drains to the inguinal (groin) nodes, which is why groin examination is essential when assessing any penile lesion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Internal iliac lymph nodes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Internal iliac nodes drain pelvic viscera. Penile skin and glans drain superficially to the inguinal nodes in the groin.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Obturator lymph nodes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Obturator nodes are deep pelvic nodes. They may be involved in advanced penile carcinoma but are not the primary drainage site.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The penis drains to the inguinal lymph nodes in the groin. This drainage pattern makes groin examination essential for any penile lesion, and inguinal nodal status is the key prognostic factor in penile carcinoma."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Inguinal nodal status is the single most important prognostic factor in penile carcinoma — that is why groin palpation is mandatory for every penile lesion."
                }
              ],
