            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In paraphimosis, the constricting band initially obstructs which pathway, triggering the vicious cycle that threatens the glans?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Arterial inflow to the glans</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Arterial compromise occurs late in the cycle. The initial obstruction is venous and lymphatic — the resulting oedema then secondarily compresses the arterial supply.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Venous and lymphatic drainage from the glans</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The trapped foreskin obstructs venous and lymphatic return first. Oedema builds, which tightens the band further, and eventually arterial flow is compromised — creating ischaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Urethral outflow, causing acute retention</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Paraphimosis does not primarily obstruct the urethra. The emergency arises from progressive vascular compromise of the glans, not from urinary retention.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nerve supply via the dorsal penile nerve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nerve compression is not the mechanism of injury in paraphimosis. The danger is progressive vascular compromise, starting with venous congestion and ending in ischaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The constricting band first obstructs venous and lymphatic drainage. This causes oedema, which tightens the band further, and eventually arterial inflow is compromised. This vicious cycle is why paraphimosis demands urgent reduction."
